WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.495
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.495 --> 00:00:04.491
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:05.988
[CLICKING]

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:15.350
LILIANE UMUBYEYI: Hi,
good morning, everyone.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:20.230
I'm happy and honored to give
this introductory session

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:21.970
on reparations.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.790
But before I start, I
would like to give you

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:28.720
my disciplinary perspective.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.680
The concept of
reparations has been

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.730
studied from different angles
and different disciplines,

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:42.250
whether it's economy,
sociology, law, philosophy.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:50.170
And I would like to give you
more details from where I speak

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.320
and I stand when I talk about
this concept of reparation.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.080
So during my PhD dissertation,
I studied mobilization

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.900
of apartheid victims
in South Africa

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.840
before American and in
South African courts.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.980
And within this dissertation,
I treated the question

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:15.550
of reparations, how this concept
was appropriated and used

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:24.610
by victims movements,
NGOs, lawyers,

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.680
and different groups of actors.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.020
So I'm not-- and
after that, I work

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:38.380
for different NGOs and human
rights organizations mainly

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.080
in sub-Saharan Africa,
an organization,

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:45.340
which were which
were implementing

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.170
programs of reparations.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:53.890
And at this stage, I'm
not going to give you

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:58.720
a presentation on the meaning
of the concept of reparations,

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.190
but rather, on how
this concept is used,

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:08.740
appropriated in different
contexts by different actors,

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:12.010
and what are the
concrete implications

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:18.640
of these multiple uses.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:25.840
I'm going to I'm going to
share my screen with you.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.330
I think it will be more
helpful since I have

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.200
different concrete examples.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:51.240
OK, so we have--

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.330
I know you have just
read the text written

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:58.770
by Pablo De Greiff on the
concept of reparations

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:04.050
and on the distinction between
different contexts, where

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:07.350
the term of reparation is used.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:12.060
In his text, Pablo De
Greiff makes the distinction

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:17.190
between international law,
where the concept of reparation

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.020
can take different forms.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.260
He talks about restitution,
compensation, rehabilitation,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.530
satisfaction, and guarantee
of non-repetition.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.760
The other context in which the
term reparation is frequently

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.520
used, according to
Pablo De Greiff,

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:43.500
is the design of reparation
programs with massive coverage.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.300
And he insists on the difference
between these different

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:54.570
contexts and
explaining how, one,

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:58.650
in the context of international
law, the concept of reparation

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:02.670
has a kind of juridical meaning.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:07.010
Whereas in the second
one, the reparation

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.780
are apprehended much more
as a political project.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:16.260
And I would like to stop
on this point to share

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:22.440
or to illustrate how these
distinctions or how different

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.020
appropriation of the
concept of reparation

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:30.270
play out in a
concrete case, which

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:36.210
is the case of apartheid victims
mobilization in South Africa.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:42.160
I would start by this example
of our project victims

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.690
mobilization.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:50.910
So as you know, at
the end of apartheid,

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:55.290
the South African government set
up a Truth and Reconciliation

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:59.250
Commission in charge of
telling the truth of the crimes

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:04.860
of apartheid, grant amnesty, if
certain conditions were made,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:10.440
and proposing recommendations
for reparations.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.420
The image on the
left illustrate one

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.710
of the session for the Truth
and Reconciliation Commission.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:25.620
So this commission was
held between 1996 and 1998.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.720
Some of the work
continued after,

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.600
but the main sessions were
held during that time.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.800
And the South African Truth
and Reconciliation Commission,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:44.940
which were interpreted apartheid
violence as human rights

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:52.170
violations related to
physical and integrity, and as

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.920
observed by Mahmood Mamdani,
and many other scholars

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.420
after this narrow interpretation
of apartheid violence

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:05.340
has the consequence
of putting aside

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.250
all the structural character
of apartheid injustices.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.610
Not considering the
social economic violence

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:23.940
of the apartheid system, and
at the level of reparations,

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:26.820
this has a consequence
since there's

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:33.420
only a limited number of people
that were considered as victims

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:39.450
of apartheid that have been
given the status of victim

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:40.440
apartheid.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:44.160
It's only 22,000 people
who could benefit

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.170
from individual reparations.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:56.430
This small nuance that I
can bring on this aspect

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:01.800
is that, in the TRC, the Truth
and Reconciliation Commission,

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:07.800
the group was aware
that, of course,

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:13.440
apartheid involved much
more discriminations.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.560
But people who have been
given the status of victims

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:25.490
and who could have access to
reparations were only 22,000.

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:32.240
In the early 2000,
in the early 2000,

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.910
a group of victims,
of apartheid victims,

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:39.560
decided to go to court
before American courts,

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:45.020
and that's the image that
we have on the right side.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:51.770
They went to court, and they
sued multinational corporations

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:56.480
that were accused of having
aided and abetted the apartheid

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.430
regime.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:02.480
In this trial, the victims
challenged the concept

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:07.280
of reparations that was
based on physical violence,

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.170
physical integrity, and
one of the main arguments

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:16.130
was to say that entire
communities were

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.800
affected by apartheid.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.190
A system of discrimination.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:26.780
Apartheid constituted a
system of discriminations

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:30.380
that affected every
aspect of the existence

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:36.200
of black populations from
the most intimate relations

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:40.440
with whom you could marry,
to the places where you could

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.919
study, where you could work,
where you could travel,

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:44.000
where you could live.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.850
So this structural character
of apartheid, and consequently,

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:53.030
the reparations
cannot be individuals,

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:58.100
cannot be apprehended
at the individual basis.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:01.970
They must take into account
this structural dimension.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:18.380
So this lawsuit has been
brought into court in 2002

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:26.330
and stayed in court until 2015.

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:29.180
But it couldn't
go further because

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.610
of the legal proceedings
and all the challenges

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:38.420
of bringing a South African
case before American courts.

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:45.000
We could go further into
further details later on,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:48.830
but I'm going to limit
myself here on this question,

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:50.780
on this aspect.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:57.100
But what is important
here to keep in mind

00:09:57.100 --> 00:10:01.870
on this aspect of reparations,
first of all, it is--

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:06.100
this case illustrates how it
is important to pay attention

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.580
on the concept, how the
concept of reparation

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:12.190
is approached and appropriated
by different actors,

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:13.850
whether it's state actors.

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:20.980
But we look, also, how
people, how movements, NGOs,

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:25.540
victims can formulate
and reformulate demands

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:30.490
in negotiating and negotiate
the sense of reparations.

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:35.200
So all that to say that
the meaning of reparation

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.690
is not something that
is fixed, that is rigid.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:42.070
It's something that
evolves, and that

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:44.050
evolves over time,
and over space,

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.470
as we see here, for what
happens in South Africa,

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:49.600
and how it goes into
American courts.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:54.220
And that is contested.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:03.180
The second element that
this case highlights

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:10.610
is that, if we understand
the concept of reparation,

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:12.980
as it has been defined by
the Truth and Reconciliation

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:16.160
Commission, and
we will compare it

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:21.530
to how it is defined by victims
movements in the case of when

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:27.500
they go to American courts,
we can see that there

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:29.350
are concrete implications.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:33.050
There are significant,
concrete implications.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:36.710
Because, on one case,
we have 22,000 people

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:39.680
who have access to reparations.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:45.350
Whereas in the second case,
their argument was to say,

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:50.710
if we take into account the
multiplicity of discrimination

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:54.800
that were inherent to
the system of apartheid,

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.380
then we have to
reconsider the number

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:01.340
of people who are victims and
who have access to reparations.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:11.150
We can see that behind this
maybe abstract definition,

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:16.730
there are significant
differences and concrete steps

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:18.540
that are important.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:24.920
I would like to move
to a second case, which

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:31.420
is the link, a second
case, but which

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:35.840
highlights the importance
of the significance

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:38.990
of the link between the
qualification of violence

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.870
and reparations.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:47.450
In other words, what kind
of violence can be repaired,

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:49.700
what kind of violence
can be repaired?

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:54.620
And what kind of victims
have access to reparations?

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:03.000
The most significant or
the most telling case

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:07.920
in this regard for me concerns
the demand for reparations

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:13.440
expressed by Namibia,
and the Ovaherero, Nama

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:17.580
in some communities to Germany.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.340
Reparations or
demanding reparations,

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:23.940
which had been
expressed concerning

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.640
the genocide against
those populations

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:30.700
at the beginning of
the 20th century.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.360
The process of claiming
this reparation

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:38.670
began in 2005 with
interested discussions

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:40.920
between Germany and Namibia.

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:45.300
And for a very
long time, Germany,

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:48.150
but also, other
former colonial powers

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:52.720
have been advancing the
inter- temporal principle.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:57.630
So this is a principle in
international law, which

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:03.160
stresses that a state is
responsible for violation

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:07.660
of international law only, if,
at the time of the violation

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:11.440
or of its continuing
effects, the state was

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:15.290
bound by the legal
provision it transgressed.

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:17.740
Well, in other
words, and this is

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:22.555
the argument, which has
been given by Germany.

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:29.470
They, at the time of--

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:32.350
at the time the
genocide was committed,

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:37.390
they were not violating
any international law.

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:40.120
And, therefore,
there's no obligation

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:44.080
to provide reparations.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:53.770
This argument has been used by
Germany and is still, somehow,

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:56.140
being used by Germany.

00:14:56.140 --> 00:15:04.020
But it also-- it has also been
used by many former colonial

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.390
powers.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:12.930
Here, you can see the image here
at the Durban Conference, which

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:18.720
is the conference, one
of the biggest conference

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:22.110
organized by the
United Nations in 2001,

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:26.430
20 years ago, on racism.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:32.790
And when many states expressed
the need for reparations

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:38.310
within this conference,
the response

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:41.310
of many colonial and
former colonial powers

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:47.450
was to advance this principle.

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:52.610
However, some
scholars, and I think

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:56.660
that is also the case for
the UN Special Rapporteur

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.990
on the question of reparations.

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:02.910
It is to say that
there is an exception.

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:10.130
There is an exception if direct
consequences of these acts

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:13.640
extend into a time when the
act and its consequences

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:17.090
are considered
international [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:22.070
For example, that means that
we could not use this for them

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:23.090
in their interpretation.

00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:28.160
We cannot use this principle
for racial discriminations, now,

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:34.130
because they are the
consequences of these facts

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:41.000
that happened at a time, where
a state had no international

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.660
legal obligation.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:49.840
Because we have direct ongoing
consequences of this fact.

00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:56.470
This case, for me--

00:16:56.470 --> 00:17:02.580
this case, for me,
it's illustrative

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:10.950
of a different important,
significant points.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:13.140
The first point that
I want to insist upon

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:17.069
is that the moment when
the facts happened,

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:19.770
the facts for which people
are asking for reparations

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:25.950
or reparation claims are made,
that moment is fundamental

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:30.030
since it determines, whether
a state can be liable

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:35.390
on the basis of international
law and as reparations.

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:39.760
This is the first key element.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:44.740
The second element, what
this case is illustrative of

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:50.650
is the paradox faced by
reparations movements since,

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:51.670
in order to demand--

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:59.260
the paradox is that they have
to use tools international--

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:01.840
if they use
international law, they

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:07.780
are constrained
to a legal system.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:11.890
And with its limits
and principles,

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:15.970
which has rendered possible,
the colonial system.

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:20.530
So they have to operate
in a system that

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:25.390
was at the basis of the
colonial phenomenon,

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:33.490
and to challenge that system on
the basis of that legal system

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:35.530
is quite constraining
and imposes

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:39.025
of the number of limits.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:47.530
And this leads me to my third
point that I would like to--

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:51.130
the third and last point that
I would like to share with you.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.860
I would even say a question.

00:18:57.860 --> 00:19:04.570
So if we think that
international law is limiting

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:07.480
because of certain principles
that have been set up

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:18.040
at a moment where colonialism
was prospering, if we think

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:21.940
that it's limiting, then,
what kind of model of justice

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:25.360
should we use or could we use if
we want to ask for reparation?

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:29.050
What could be the alternative
to international law?

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:41.640
One of the cases that could
be that is interesting

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:54.500
here is the case of
reparations in Canada.

00:19:54.500 --> 00:20:01.130
So before I address the
image in the dissertation

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:03.620
that you see here, I would
like to just give you

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:08.090
a little bit of background.

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:15.380
So as you may know, from the
late 19th century to 1996,

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:18.350
the government of
Canada operated

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.830
the Indian residential
school system

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:25.610
with the goal of assimilating
indigenous children

00:20:25.610 --> 00:20:31.610
by stripping them of their
traditions, customs, values,

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:32.990
languages.

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:39.680
And the children were subjected
to different physical abuses,

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:44.150
sexual and emotional abuses.

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:48.140
And in the early '90s, former
students of this system

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:58.770
decided to litigate to ask for
reparations to the government,

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:03.140
but also, to the churches that
were involved in this system.

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:09.260
And they denounced in a
lawsuit, and this lawsuit

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:14.590
led to a settlement
with the government,

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:16.115
a settlement of $5 billion.

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:26.570
And that settlement also
involved reparations,

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:31.640
which were not only
financial reparations,

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:36.680
but also, the acknowledgment of
the past wrongs, compensation,

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:40.190
rehabilitative
measures, including

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:42.590
physical and
psychological health

00:21:42.590 --> 00:21:47.510
services, legal services,
educational support,

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:50.450
and the establishment of
the Truth and Reconciliation

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:52.100
Commission.

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:59.270
And the chief investigator
involved in the negotiation

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:04.340
of this settlement has explained
that it was possible --

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:09.830
That settlement was only
possible by setting aside

00:22:09.830 --> 00:22:15.020
conventional legal
frameworks of tort

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:18.590
and civil law in favor
of indigenous law

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:22.610
and legal traditions,
and my point here

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:40.380
is to show what can be
achieved, if we decide,

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:45.720
if we follow alternative
paths to international law.

00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:54.710
And for me-- and this is
more a question and, I think,

00:22:54.710 --> 00:23:00.710
something that we we'll address
in the different workshops

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:08.030
or the different presentation
that will follow in the modules

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:12.680
is to understand what extent
the transitional justice

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:18.290
framework could be useful in
addressing, or responding,

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:23.600
or addressing colonial
violences, injustices.

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:25.220
Colonial, but also, slavery.

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:33.960
Why do I bring this question of
transitional justice framework?

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:41.070
I think about transitional
justice framework,

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:49.400
because the original idea behind
transitional justice is that

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:50.520
legal practitioners..

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:53.210
And that's in the late '90s.

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:58.550
Some legal practitioners
expressed the need

00:23:58.550 --> 00:24:02.210
to question the centrality
of the criminal trial

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:05.990
as a way to respond
to mass violence,

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:09.430
and there was a desire among
these legal practitioners

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:11.600
to look at the
mechanisms that were

00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:17.510
used in different mechanisms
for resolving conflicts

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:25.700
in the African states,
in Asian states,

00:24:25.700 --> 00:24:29.870
South America, but non-Western,
a non-Western concept

00:24:29.870 --> 00:24:35.540
of seeking individual
responsibility.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:41.660
And there were-- and
within this interrogation

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:45.740
emerged the idea of
using some concept

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:49.280
and mechanisms, the Truth and
Reconciliation Commission.

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:57.800
Of course, that was the
original idea in the '80s.

00:24:57.800 --> 00:25:02.150
But after that, the
transitional justice framework

00:25:02.150 --> 00:25:10.620
has evolved and has come
to include criminal trials.

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:14.970
I'm not here to say
if it's a good--

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:20.340
I mean, if it's a
good or bad evolution.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:24.900
But we know that the
field has evolved, and has

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:32.760
come to include this evolution,
and has come to be much more--

00:25:32.760 --> 00:25:37.140
it's much more used by
international organizations,

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:40.770
NGO, United Nations, with
all the technicalities

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:42.600
that it involves.

00:25:42.600 --> 00:25:45.660
But at the beginning
of this interrogation,

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:50.910
there was the need to
question the centrality

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:53.520
of the Western use of trials.

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:05.190
Yeah, what I found
interesting in these debates

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:09.150
about reparation and about
reparations for colonialism

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:15.090
is to see if these models
that are, most of the time,

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:17.760
used in the global
South, if they

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:21.840
can be used to rethink the
way we think about trials,

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:26.340
the way we think about
litigation regarding

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:33.590
colonialism and slavery
in the Western societies.

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:39.320
So all this to say that
the concept of reparation

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:40.170
is not a--

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:49.310
it's not something--

00:26:49.310 --> 00:26:53.840
I mean, to conclude, it's
not something that is rigid.

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:55.700
It's not something that
has a fixed meaning.

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:59.900
It's something that is evolving
over time and over space.

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:03.050
But it is also a
concept that is evolving

00:27:03.050 --> 00:27:06.680
in the midst of
many transformations

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:09.350
of international law, of
transitional injustice,

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:16.700
of other mechanisms and
practices that are also

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:26.840
inherited from the history of
the legal systems and practices

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:28.460
of the resolving conflicts.

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:35.850
Yes, so that's it.

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:46.200
I think that we can dig.

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:52.440
We can go deeper into some
aspects within the Q&amp;A

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:56.410
and, of course,
within the modules.

00:27:56.410 --> 00:27:58.160
Thank you.

